\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Model and Training.}
We conduct \emph{continued pretraining} of Qwen3-0.6B on a contaminated corpus (raw pretrained perplexity: 22.97).
We use AdamW with learning rate $5 \times 10^{-5}$ and cosine schedule with warmup, training for 6{,}103 steps (${\sim}0.26$ epochs) with micro-batch size 4 and gradient accumulation 4 (effective batch size 16), max sequence length 2048, and FP16 mixed precision on an NVIDIA RTX 6000 Pro (96GB).

\paragraph{Data.}
The training corpus contains 247{,}164 samples (132M+ tokens), comprising 60\% human-written text from FineWeb \citep{penedo2024fineweb} and 40\% synthetic text generated by the training model itself (Qwen3-0.6B), simulating a self-consumption scenario where a model trains on its own outputs \citep{shumailov2024model}.
Synthetic samples are produced via prompted continuation from human text prefixes.
Each sample carries a contamination score $q_i \in [0, 1]$ assigned by the detector (\S\ref{sec:detector}).

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
All methods are evaluated on held-out perplexity using sliding-window evaluation (stride 1024, max length 2048, FP16) on a balanced held-out set of 4{,}077{,}068 tokens containing equal proportions of real and synthetic text.
Every method is evaluated on the identical held-out set for fair comparison.

\subsection{Contamination Detector}
\label{sec:detector}

We use a TF-IDF + Logistic Regression classifier as our contamination detector.
Despite its simplicity, it achieves AUC\,=\,0.939 and accuracy\,=\,0.858.
Critically, we prioritize \emph{calibration} of the continuous $q$-scores over classification accuracy: raw ECE of 0.24 is reduced to 0.048 via temperature scaling ($T = 0.204$).
The resulting score distribution (mean\,=\,0.600, std\,=\,0.374, median\,=\,0.707) places 46.3\% of samples in the ambiguous mid-range $0.2 < q < 0.8$.

This design choice is deliberate: our reweighting function $w_i = (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$ operates on continuous scores, so well-calibrated probabilities ensure that weights faithfully reflect contamination likelihood.
This contrasts with \citet{drayson2025}, who employ a ModernBERT-based detector (AUC\,=\,0.986); we show that a lightweight detector suffices when scores are properly calibrated (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare our \textbf{Adaptive} method ($\tau = 0.8$, $n_{\min} = 2$) against four baselines, all using the same detector scores and training configuration:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Uniform}: $w_i = 1$ for all samples; no contamination handling.
\item \textbf{Grid-$b$}: fixed $b \in \{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0\}$, with the best $b$ selected post hoc from validation perplexity. This requires $6\times$ the training cost.
\item \textbf{ESS-only}: $b^* = \max\{b : \text{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}\}$, i.e., the maximum downweighting permitted by the effective sample size constraint alone. This \emph{maximum intervention} strategy forms the direct counterpoint to our minimum intervention approach.
\item \textbf{Golden Ratio}: $w = 1/\varphi \approx 0.618$ for synthetic samples ($q > 0.5$), $w = 1.0$ otherwise \citep{he2025golden}. This oracle baseline assumes knowledge of the true contamination proportion.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Experimental Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

Experiments are organized into four groups:

\noindent\textbf{(1) Main comparison} (3 seeds: 42, 123, 456): Adaptive, Grid-best, Uniform, ESS-only, and Golden Ratio under identical conditions.

\noindent\textbf{(2) $\tau$ sensitivity} (seed 42): $\tau \in \{0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 0.95\}$, characterizing the effective range of the quality threshold and its interaction with the adaptive exponent $b^*$.

\noindent\textbf{(3) $n_{\min}$ sensitivity} (seed 42): $n_{\min} \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$ at batch size 4, examining how the stability constraint's stringency affects reweighting behavior.

\noindent\textbf{(4) Batch size} (seed 42): batch sizes 4 and 8 with $n_{\min} = 2$, testing robustness of the ESS constraint across different effective sample sizes.
